    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research fork-join quicksort: keep generation serial and deterministic, then use bounded parallel tasks for sufficiently large recursive partitions, with a sequential cutoff. Use a duplicate-safe three-way partition and a depth/imbalance fallback; do not call the shared randum_w/randum_z generator concurrently. Measure end-to-end performance, including sorting, validation, and MD5.\",\"B\":\"Research parallel partitioning within quicksort: for large ranges, classify chunks into less-than, equal-to, and greater-than regions, compute per-worker counts and disjoint prefix offsets, then scatter through reusable scratch storage before recursively sorting the outer regions. Include safe handling for duplicates, uneven partitions, and small ranges; measure the memory and synchronization overhead as well as runtime.\",\"C\":\"Research a sample-driven multiway quicksort: choose splitters from the current data, partition large ranges into buckets with per-worker counts and disjoint scatter, and recursively sort the buckets. Handle equal keys and badly imbalanced buckets explicitly, with a fallback or sequential cutoff. Treat this as a separate candidate and validate it for arbitrary valid N, not just observed cases.\",\"G\":\"Use the unchanged measured baseline as the reference and compare each complete candidate under the same correctness and timing protocol. Integrate only a candidate that is correct and shows a repeatable end-to-end improvement; if combining compatible ideas, remeasure the combined program rather than assuming gains add. Preserve the input behavior, generated values, sorted integer sequence, checksum bytes, and output format. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler/toolchain, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The source is a serial C quicksort over an array generated from the input N, followed by a sortedness check and an MD5 of the sorted array. The measured baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.0743118245 seconds (three samples) and score 0.5. The target has 4 ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU, motivating bounded CPU-parallel sorting experiments. This is a single baseline observation, not evidence that candidate gains will combine; preserve the deterministic generator and exact sorted bytes, and evaluate complete programs.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"e57bdc6f82d3da2ce6dc6e8f1aa15a792720e31f1963955d5a3ad1b5f82fac4b\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research fork-join quicksort: keep generation serial and deterministic, then use bounded parallel tasks for sufficiently large recursive partitions, with a sequential cutoff. Use a duplicate-safe three-way partition and a depth/imbalance fallback; do not call the shared randum_w/randum_z generator concurrently. Measure end-to-end performance, including sorting, validation, and MD5.\",\"B\":\"Research parallel partitioning within quicksort: for large ranges, classify chunks into less-than, equal-to, and greater-than regions, compute per-worker counts and disjoint prefix offsets, then scatter through reusable scratch storage before recursively sorting the outer regions. Include safe handling for duplicates, uneven partitions, and small ranges; measure the memory and synchronization overhead as well as runtime.\",\"C\":\"Research a sample-driven multiway quicksort: choose splitters from the current data, partition large ranges into buckets with per-worker counts and disjoint scatter, and recursively sort the buckets. Handle equal keys and badly imbalanced buckets explicitly, with a fallback or sequential cutoff. Treat this as a separate candidate and validate it for arbitrary valid N, not just observed cases.\",\"G\":\"Use the unchanged measured baseline as the reference and compare each complete candidate under the same correctness and timing protocol. Integrate only a candidate that is correct and shows a repeatable end-to-end improvement; if combining compatible ideas, remeasure the combined program rather than assuming gains add. Preserve the input behavior, generated values, sorted integer sequence, checksum bytes, and output format. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler/toolchain, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The source is a serial C quicksort over an array generated from the input N, followed by a sortedness check and an MD5 of the sorted array. The measured baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.0743118245 seconds (three samples) and score 0.5. The target has 4 ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU, motivating bounded CPU-parallel sorting experiments. This is a single baseline observation, not evidence that candidate gains will combine; preserve the deterministic generator and exact sorted bytes, and evaluate complete programs.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"93110787159748d4b05232bd50eb3032\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"e57bdc6f82d3da2ce6dc6e8f1aa15a792720e31f1963955d5a3ad1b5f82fac4b\",\"session_id\":\"bb7a58941e74486ba24267f2f526de8f\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"34c3ae52c7f8bb7e1e83e7b349050005e885272a087f168f4f386a7afb6139d6\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":12532,\"output_tokens\":5887,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":5439}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":18419,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
