      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Use the measured-fastest radix candidate (aa19cdb376bf40ea; 18.49 ms) as the research starting point. Tune a general integer radix sort—such as pass width and histogram layout—while preserving full signed ordering, all input sizes, allocation safety, and the existing generation/checksum behavior. Verify output against the oracle; do not assume the recorded timing establishes correctness.\",\"B\":\"Investigate time outside the sort, especially whether the generated-case loop can be optimized while producing exactly the same values and preserving the RNG sequence and floating-point conversion semantics. Keep checksum and output behavior unchanged. The SIMD sortedness scan showed little measured benefit, so pursue it only if repeatable measurements justify it.\",\"C\":\"Explore parallelizing the radix approach rather than the slower measured parallel quicksort variants. For sufficiently large arrays, evaluate per-worker histograms and race-free prefix offsets/scatter with a serial fallback and a measured crossover threshold; cap parallelism at the available four cores. Preserve general integer ordering and verify correctness for every tested size.\",\"G\":\"Treat the radix candidate at 18.49 ms as the provisional best, not as a proven-correct final integration. Integrate tree outputs one at a time, validate each against the oracle, and measure the combined program on the same target and harness. Retain a combined change only when its own correctness and timing are confirmed; do not add individual gains arithmetically or carry over slower OpenMP quicksort changes without evidence.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline is correct at 74.31 ms. The measured radix candidate is fastest at 18.49 ms (about 4.0x the baseline); the sampled parallel quicksort variants measured 32.91 and 36.16 ms, while other comparison-sort and OpenMP attempts were about 62–74 ms. This favors refining radix sorting, checking whether remaining runtime is outside the sort, and testing parallel radix cautiously on the four-core ARM64 target. Candidate correctness is not established by the supplied baseline correctness record, so require oracle validation before integration.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"eec9e5702bdf003e1c0cd10683ef1989902120b526d60b4588f31dcc6c29c4c1\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Use the measured-fastest radix candidate (aa19cdb376bf40ea; 18.49 ms) as the research starting point. Tune a general integer radix sort—such as pass width and histogram layout—while preserving full signed ordering, all input sizes, allocation safety, and the existing generation/checksum behavior. Verify output against the oracle; do not assume the recorded timing establishes correctness.\",\"B\":\"Investigate time outside the sort, especially whether the generated-case loop can be optimized while producing exactly the same values and preserving the RNG sequence and floating-point conversion semantics. Keep checksum and output behavior unchanged. The SIMD sortedness scan showed little measured benefit, so pursue it only if repeatable measurements justify it.\",\"C\":\"Explore parallelizing the radix approach rather than the slower measured parallel quicksort variants. For sufficiently large arrays, evaluate per-worker histograms and race-free prefix offsets/scatter with a serial fallback and a measured crossover threshold; cap parallelism at the available four cores. Preserve general integer ordering and verify correctness for every tested size.\",\"G\":\"Treat the radix candidate at 18.49 ms as the provisional best, not as a proven-correct final integration. Integrate tree outputs one at a time, validate each against the oracle, and measure the combined program on the same target and harness. Retain a combined change only when its own correctness and timing are confirmed; do not add individual gains arithmetically or carry over slower OpenMP quicksort changes without evidence.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline is correct at 74.31 ms. The measured radix candidate is fastest at 18.49 ms (about 4.0x the baseline); the sampled parallel quicksort variants measured 32.91 and 36.16 ms, while other comparison-sort and OpenMP attempts were about 62–74 ms. This favors refining radix sorting, checking whether remaining runtime is outside the sort, and testing parallel radix cautiously on the four-core ARM64 target. Candidate correctness is not established by the supplied baseline correctness record, so require oracle validation before integration.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"4d311de886ec494ba21c7aa9c5b46e01\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"eec9e5702bdf003e1c0cd10683ef1989902120b526d60b4588f31dcc6c29c4c1\",\"session_id\":\"bb7a58941e74486ba24267f2f526de8f\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"0c4883feab92de728554849b65e06500e0c7242f2e4b5da351e87ad4443cc3aa\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":24415,\"output_tokens\":3260,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":2830}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":27675,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
